## Power, atmosphere, water, and food

| Parameter | Evidence and context | Treatment and game value |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Solar input and tile characteristics | [NASA Mars surface power white paper](https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/mars-surface-power-generation-challenges-and-considerations.pdf) discusses seasonal illumination and dust; [NASA SAWS study, 2018](https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20200004325/downloads/NASA%20TM2018-219945.pdf) uses about 590 W/m² above the Martian atmosphere | Medium for generic surface scale, low for a site forecast. Game assumes 350 W/m² peak at the panels, 28 m² effective area per tile, 23.5% conversion, and a half-sine daylight profile over 12 Mars hours. A clean tile peaks at 2.30 kW and generates about 18.1 kWh per clear sol before wear. |
| Dust and persistent soiling | [Opportunity 2018 storm](https://science.nasa.gov/resource/on-mars-light-is-energy/) reported opacity 10.5 and 21 Wh for the rover on 10 June. The NASA surface power paper also describes InSight’s accumulation. | High for the observations. Training settings use 45% transmission during a four-sol storm every 35 sols beginning at elapsed sol 14. Each storm sol deposits 4.5 percentage points of panel soiling, capped at 35%. Cleaning resets deposits on one tile. Panel condition declines 0.2 points per sol to a 50% floor and can be restored by repair. |
| Battery and power allocation | Basic energy accounting and NASA surface power considerations | Training equipment estimates. Start at 67 kWh, with 120 kWh of capacity per Central Module and 94% one-way charge and discharge efficiency. Demand priority is base load, CO₂ removal, O₂ generation, water recycling, then active greenhouse lights. Battery power-rate and thermal restrictions are folded into the equipment assumptions. |
| Daily crew rates | [NASA deep-space life-support analysis, 2016](https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20160005782/downloads/20160005782.pdf), table 1, adult crew member per Earth day | High as reference values. Each Earth day per crew member uses 0.84 kg O₂, 0.62 kg food solids, and 5.31 kg water, and produces 1.00 kg CO₂. Multiply each by 1.02749 for one sol. |
| Water recovery | [NASA ISS water recovery milestone, 2023](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/station/iss-research/nasa-achieves-water-recovery-milestone-on-international-space-station/) demonstrated 98% total recovery. The 2016 life-support table lists candidate water streams. | High for the ISS result. The game recovers 90% of a modeled 5.05 kg of recoverable water per crew member per Earth day, limited by the water issued that step and by available recycling power. It assumes same-step recycling and combines water streams. With electrolysis replacing breathing oxygen, baseline net draw is about 3.51 kg per sol for two people. |
| Electrolysis and air revitalization | [NASA ECLSS reference](https://www.nasa.gov/reference/environmental-control-and-life-support-systems-eclss/) describes water electrolysis and CO₂ removal as separate systems | Training capacities and loads. Base habitat 0.25 kW, generator up to 0.42 kW at 0.075 kg O₂/h, scrubber 0.19 kW up to 0.1 kg CO₂/h, recycler 0.15 kW. Electrolysis consumes 1.125 kg of water per kg of oxygen by stoichiometry. Hydrogen and removed CO₂ leave the tracked loop. MOXIE used a different process on Mars and demonstrated up to 12 g O₂/hour. [NASA MOXIE results, 2023](https://www.nasa.gov/solar-system/nasas-oxygen-generating-experiment-moxie-completes-mars-mission/) |
| Atmosphere stocks and emergency thresholds | Closed-cabin maintenance grounded in NASA life-support analysis | Game calibration. O₂ stock starts at 6 kg and is capped at 8 kg. CO₂ starts at 0.50 kg with a 0.45 kg scrubber floor. O₂ below 4 kg and CO₂ at or above 3.6 kg warn players. Air fails on unmet oxygen demand or CO₂ at 7 kg. The UI reports stock mass, so these values imply no cabin pressure or occupational exposure limit. |
| Greenhouse | [NASA Veggie research summary, 2019](https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20190028955/downloads/20190028955.pdf) describes a 33-day terminal harvest for ISS red romaine. The [OSDR study](https://osdr.nasa.gov/bio/repo/data/studies/OSD-267) links the experiment data. | Medium for the example crop timescale, low for a Mars greenhouse prediction. A garden takes 30 fully tended, powered daylight-equivalent sols. Lights draw 0.4 kW for up to half a sol and consume 0.55 kg/sol of net process water at full operation. Each cycle adds 2.4 kg of dry food equivalent. The crop represents a supplemental mix, with seeds, carbon feed and nutrients implicit in the kit. |

A simulation step lasts 1.02749 Earth hours. Energy charged is surplus kWh multiplied by 0.94. Energy removed from storage is the unmet load in kWh divided by 0.94. Stored charge is clamped after allocation. For each supply, starting stock minus amount issued plus any modeled production, recovery, and landing-site transfers equals ending stock. Slower or unpowered systems perform proportionately less work. Crop food above base capacity is discarded. Delivered supply overflow is retained at the landing site.

## Radiation, communications, and cargo

| Parameter | Source and context | Treatment and game value |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Chronic radiation | [Hassler et al., Science 2014](https://dspace.library.uu.nl/bitstream/1874/309142/1/Science_2014_Hassler_.pdf), table 1. Curiosity RAD measured 0.64 ± 0.12 mSv per Earth day on the surface of Gale crater in the studied period. | High for measured context, low as a Noctis forecast. Use 0.64 mSv/Earth day as training background. Indoor background is 80% of that. The display uses a conservative shared crew estimate based on whether anyone was outside. |
| Acute solar events and shielding | [NASA RAD measurement summary, 2013](https://science.nasa.gov/resource/radiation-measurements-on-mars/) and [NASA Curiosity shielding observations, 2021](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/mars-science-laboratory/how-nasas-curiosity-rover-is-making-mars-safer-for-astronauts/) | Fictional two-sol events recur every 39 sols starting at elapsed sol 28. Added dose is 4 mSv/sol before applying 0.2 sheltered or 1.4 unsheltered EVA factors. The 9 mSv episode stop is a precautionary mission rule. Accumulated exposure stays visible and is distinct from an episode stop. |
| Signal delay | [NASA Mars communications white paper, 2023 architecture review](https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/mars-communications-disruption-and-delay.pdf) notes a one-way delay that can reach 21–23 minutes. [Perseverance landing facts, 2021](https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/press_kits/mars_2020/landing/quick_facts/) give 11 min 22 s one-way for landing day. | High for reported mission context. Next-sol check-in is a mission-scheduling abstraction that makes requesting ahead useful at game speed. Crew safety decisions remain local. |
| Earth launch and transfer | [NASA Mars mission timeline](https://science.nasa.gov/planetary-science/programs/mars-exploration/mission-timeline/) describes Perseverance’s roughly seven-month transfer and the roughly 26-month interval between energy-efficient opportunities. [NIAC Phase I wave-rotor nuclear thermal and electric concept, 2023](https://www.nasa.gov/general/new-class-of-bimodal-ntp-nep-with-a-wave-rotor-topping-cycle-enabling-fast-transit-to-mars/) proposed 45 days. | High for the flown mission and for what the concept proposed. The vehicle, 5-sol windows every 84 sols, and 70-sol transit are fictional settings. Specific impulse alone does not establish a transfer time. The training schedule represents launch, cruise, landing, and unloading as distinct steps. |
| Payload and infrastructure | Generic trade-off between mass, volume, time, and resources | Game settings. Four slots, 1,150 kg, 7 m³. Food and water package volumes are 0.0035 and 0.0012 m³/kg. Initial food and water are 175 kg and 460 kg. Base limits are 240 kg food and 650 kg water. Each Storage Module adds 140 kg and 420 kg. Module crate mass, volume, credit cost, durations and budgets are scenario settings in src/config.mjs. |
| Training success and evacuation | Scenario rules | Five-sols supply margin plus safe air and battery for mission success. A sustained essential shortage adds emergency steps and triggers evacuation at 36. Steps with healthy operation clear debt twice as fast. Credit and science awards recognize completed milestones. They are fictional planning rewards. |

The campaign uses embedded observations, assumptions, and deterministic forecasts. Source links are available for follow-up with internet access.

